# Mandatory Function Wrapper Rule

Every JavaScript/TypeScript file must wrap all executable logic with the global `wrapKDB`.

Exception: the file that defines the global `wrapKDB` factory itself is excluded.

The canonical definition module is **`KDB3/js/core/bootstrap/globalUtils/wrapKDB.js`** (AMD). It exposes the callable `wrapKDB(modulePath)` factory and is exempt from requiring `const fw = wrapKDB(...)` and from wrapping its own internal functions with `fw`. **`KDB3/js/core/bootstrap/globalUtils/publishGlobUtils.js`** is the only module that assigns `wrapKDB` (and other allowed globals) to `globalThis` / `window` / `window.top`; that file contains host-publish wiring only (no `fw` required there). All other JavaScript files under `KDB3/js/` follow the full wrapper rules.

## File-level wrapper

After imports and directives, define a local wrapper named `fw`.

```js
const fw = wrapKDB("path/to/currentFile.js");
```

The path must identify the current file or logical module in error logs.

Allowed before `fw`:

- imports
- type-only imports
- comments
- `"use strict"` or similar directives

No executable logic may appear before `fw`.

## Core rule

Every executable function must be wrapped with `fw`.

This includes:

- named functions
- anonymous functions
- arrow functions
- async functions
- object methods
- class methods
- exported functions
- nested functions
- callbacks
- iterator callbacks
- event handlers
- promise callbacks
- timer callbacks

Required pattern:

```js
const renderBars = fw("topNRenderer.renderBars", function (container, data) {
  // implementation
});
```

Async:

```js
const loadData = fw("topNService.loadData", async function () {
  // implementation
});
```

Export:

```js
export const renderBars = fw("topNRenderer.renderBars", function () {
  // implementation
});
```

## Object and class methods

Object methods must be wrapped directly.

```js
const topNRenderer = {
  render: fw("topNRenderer.render", function () {
    // implementation
  })
};
```

Class methods must either be wrapped class fields or delegate only to a wrapped implementation.

```js
class TopNController {
  initialize = fw("TopNController.initialize", function () {
    // implementation
  });
}
```

Alternative:

```js
class TopNController {
  initialize() {
    return initializeTopNController.apply(this, arguments);
  }
}

const initializeTopNController = fw("TopNController.initialize", function () {
  // implementation
});
```

The delegating method must contain no additional logic.

## Callback rule

Wrapping an outer function is not enough.

Every callback inside a wrapped function must also be wrapped.

This applies to:

- `forEach`
- `map`
- `filter`
- `reduce`
- `some`
- `every`
- `find`
- `findIndex`
- `sort`
- `flatMap`
- `addEventListener`
- jQuery `.on()`
- `.then()`
- `.catch()`
- `setTimeout`
- `setInterval`

Correct:

```js
items.forEach(
  fw("items.forEach:initializeItem", function (item, index) {
    initializeItem(item, index);
  })
);
```

Incorrect:

```js
items.forEach(item => {
  initializeItem(item);
});
```

## Forbidden patterns

Unwrapped functions are forbidden.

```js
function myFunction() {}
const myFunction = function () {};
const myFunction = () => {};
items.map(item => item.id);
button.addEventListener("click", function () {});
```

## Naming rule

The name passed to `fw` must be specific, stable and searchable.

Good:

```js
fw("topNRenderer.renderBars", function () {});
fw("topNController.initialize", function () {});
fw("filterState.applySelection", function () {});
fw("widgets.forEach:initializeWidget", function () {});
fw("button.click:applyFilter", function () {});
```

Bad:

```js
fw("callback", function () {});
fw("handler", function () {});
fw("anonymous", function () {});
fw("function", function () {});
fw("test", function () {});
fw("temp", function () {});
```

## Error handling

The wrapper is responsible for centralized error handling.

Do not add local `try/catch` unless the function can recover, convert the error to a controlled fallback, add meaningful context and rethrow, or perform cleanup before rethrowing.

Local `try/catch` must not swallow errors, log only, hide the stack, or replace wrapper behavior.

The wrapper must preserve:

- file/module name
- function/method/callback name
- original error message
- original stack, where available

## Non-executable declarations

Types, interfaces, constants and static config objects do not need wrapping unless they contain executable functions.

## Completion criteria

Code is incomplete if any executable function, method or callback is unwrapped, if `fw` is missing, if `wrapKDB` does not receive the file/module path, or if generic wrapper names are used.
